            <h1>A Taste Of <span class="accent">Philippines</span></h1>
            <p>Filipino cooking is a table shaped by trade winds and colonization, island geography,
                and generations of home cooks making do with what the land and sea provided. Malay,
                Chinese, Spanish, and American influences layer over a native foundation of rice, fish
                sauce, vinegar, and coconut — producing a cuisine that leans sour, salty, and savory
                rather than sweet or heavily spiced. Every region carries its own version of the
                national table: Luzon's love of vinegar and soy, the Visayas' grilled and roasted meats,
                Mindanao's coconut and chili-forward dishes shaped by trade with nearby Southeast Asia.
                What follows is a small selection of the dishes that define it.</p>
