            <h1>A Taste Of <span class="accent">Japan</span></h1>
            <p>Japanese cooking is built on restraint — the idea that a dish should let its main
                ingredient speak, not cover it up. Dashi, a broth of kombu and bonito flakes, forms
                the quiet backbone of countless dishes, while rice and soy sauce tie regional
                cuisines together from Hokkaido's seafood to Kyushu's pork-heavy ramen. Seasonality
                guides the menu as much as flavor does, and presentation is treated as part of the
                cooking itself. What follows is a small selection of the dishes that define it.</p>
